I’ll inspect the metadata, pattern, and reusable analysis tools first, then build a physically constrained phase inventory and preserve a replayable GSAS-II checkpoint before testing focused alternatives. I’ll keep exact-formula source records and any constructed analogues health-screened as required.

The chemistry points first to calcium tungstates, but the phase count is deliberately unspecified. I’m now inventorying all Ca–W–O source records (including non-1:1 products and precursor remnants) and extracting the observed peak families before selecting structures; a poor database record will be challenged with another polymorph rather than treated as evidence against its formula.

Peak families already establish a plausible two-phase closure: scheelite CaWO₄ accounts for the dominant 18.43/28.54/31.27/34.01/39.03/46.93° series, while ambient monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the independent 22.01/22.94/23.42/24.23/26.44/33.10/33.43° families. The high-pressure fergusonite CaWO₄ record is positionally incompatible; among WO₃ records, the conventional monoclinic model matches the observed triplet much better than the tetragonal variants. I’m moving these serious candidates into matched GSAS-II refinements and will test whether CaO or another calcium tungstate is actually needed after residual closure.

The first replay-stable two-phase checkpoint is physical and positive (CaWO₄ ≈86.4 wt%, WO₃ ≈13.6 wt%), but its residuals are not yet mature: the 004/101 and related CaWO₄ intensity ratios are strongly directional, and several narrower residual families remain. I’ve preserved that checkpoint. I’m now doing the required one-at-a-time size/strain comparisons and compact texture-axis tests from the same saved state; bound or collapsed branches will be rejected rather than allowed to contaminate the deliverable.

The challenge matrix is still running its independent replays. Early branches are converging without frozen parameters; I’m waiting for the matched axis/profile comparisons to finish before selecting any added term.

The slower branches are the profile/texture combinations that need Marquardt damping, but the baseline checkpoint remains untouched. I’m allowing each branch to complete one reload-and-replay cycle so apparent improvements that do not survive reopening are excluded.

The CaWO₄ width term is family-supported and physically finite (~77 nm apparent coherent size), while strain-only alternatives ran negative and are invalid. The [001] and [111] texture leaders remain nearly tied, so the prescribed single nearby-axis expansion and cross-seeding are in progress; WO₃ texture is effectively neutral and will not be retained.

The retained checkpoint now has a stable [110] CaWO₄ texture term plus a finite 79 nm coherent-size term, with only common U/W active and no bounds; its replayed Rwp is 10.14%. Residual peaks at 18.74, 19.29, 22.01, 30.71, 32.18, and 37.53° remain coherent, so exact-source and precursor-remnant challenges are still necessary before any non-1:1 analogue is considered.

The exact-source tests are decisive on topology: fergusonite CaWO₄ fails badly (Rwp 42.4%), orthorhombic and small-cell WO₃ are worse, and the health-passed monoclinic WO₃ record ties the alternative standard-setting record without its short O–O contacts. CaO refines to a small positive ~1.3 wt% contribution, but its evidence is marginal and will undergo broadening/texture/removal challenges. The low-angle residual families remain unexplained, so I’m now enumerating charge-balanced non-1:1 Ca–W–O formulas and inspecting analogue parents as directed.

The analogue search found a compelling, chemically conservative parent: P2₁/c Ca₃OsO₆. Isovalent octahedral Os(VI)→W(VI) preserves the Ca/O framework, and its predicted peaks map almost one-for-one onto the unexplained families (after the common zero correction): 18.89→18.74, 19.49→19.29, 22.16→22.01, 31.62→31.41, 45.21→45.13, and 57.16→57.02°. I constructed both this model and a structurally distinct P2₁ Ca₃UO₆-derived challenge; both pass the required geometry/occupancy audit. I’ll refine the P2₁/c scale first, with its cell fixed, before allowing its cell or any profile term to move.

The P2₁/n Ca₃WO₆ analogue is confirmed, not merely suggestive: its scale is positive on independent replay, its cell refines only ~1% from the Ca₃OsO₆ parent, and it lowers Rwp from 10.14% to 6.86%. The modeled crystalline fractions are currently about 80.25% CaWO₄, 11.09% WO₃, and 8.67% Ca₃WO₆. I’m freezing a schema-complete, replayed checkpoint now before optional source/profile/texture challenges continue.

The three-phase checkpoint is safely exported and health-passes. I’m now repeating the profile challenge at the completed inventory (neutral/size/strain/combined for all three phases) because adding Ca₃WO₆ changes how common and phase-specific widths trade; only positive, replay-stable terms will survive.

The completed-inventory width tests are clean: finite isotropic coherent-size terms are supported for all three phases, and the collective replay is stable at Rwp 6.80% (approximately 79.1/11.3/9.6 wt%). Strain-only or combined branches either fit less well or drive strain to zero, so strain is pruned. I’m now applying the compact symmetry-aware texture basis at this collective checkpoint, including neutral deletions.

The collective size model remains valid while texture branches run. I’m retaining the previously selected CaWO₄ [110] term unless another axis wins after all three phase widths are active; WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ texture terms must beat their neutral deletions and remain away from bounds.

The source-topology comparison is now at matched three-phase elaboration: the conventional monoclinic WO₃, the standard-setting monoclinic record with short O–O contacts, and the lower-symmetry triclinic record are each receiving the same sizes and compact texture basis. Orthorhombic WO₃ was already decisively poorer and is not being expanded further.

The matched source runs are slower because each compact-axis branch independently reloads and replays all nuisance terms. The valid three-phase checkpoint remains untouched; no phase inventory decision depends on these optional branches unless a source wins both neutral and elaborated comparisons.

No failures have surfaced, but some lower-symmetry branches need many damped cycles. I’m letting the current matched run finish rather than truncating it mid-comparison; this is the last broad source matrix, after which only targeted stabilization and output verification remain.

The three-phase model is physically closed and already has a valid checkpoint. I’m finishing the matched source, trace-phase, background, and instrument challenges now, then I’ll replay the exact final project and regenerate all deliverables from it.

The first compact WO₃-source comparison was invalid because a shared profile term hit its lower bound. I’m repeating that comparison with the same cross-seeded common profile values for every source; this isolates the structural topology and prevents a bound artifact from deciding the source.

The mature tests strongly favor refining polarization (Rwp 6.79% → 2.86%) while the surface-roughness alternative is highly correlated and unstable. CaO refines to only ~0.18 wt% with a negligible fit gain, so I’m testing whether it has independent peak-family support before deciding whether to retain it.

Polarization is replay-stable and its deletion restores the poor fit; six background terms are also clearly insufficient. Once polarization is included, the two minor-phase texture ratios refine essentially to 1.000, so I’m pruning those unsupported terms and replaying one-term width/texture deletions before freezing the final model.

The pruning replay is still running through the one-term deletions. The current candidate remains valid; these branches are only checking which profile/texture terms deserve to survive in the submitted project.

The final retained model now has only supported terms: three phase sizes, CaWO₄ [110] March texture, an eight-term smooth background, zero shift, U/W, and polarization. Strain, roughness, asymmetry, X, and both minor-phase texture terms are neutral; I’m now replaying this exact full-range model under `final.gpx` and exporting directly from the reopened project.

The final CIFs all pass the required geometry/occupancy health screen. The only trace-phase candidate, CaO, fails the two-independent-family gate and is excluded; its conditional ~0.13 wt% estimate is documented as unreliable rather than folded into the normalized fractions.

Completed the full-range GSAS-II refinement.

